      "math_academy_id": "q-183999",
      "sequence_position": 15,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=8{m}^{3}+12{m}^{2}+10m+2,$ where $m\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8{m}^{3}+12{m}^{2}+10m+2 \\\\  & =2(4{m}^{3}+6{m}^{2}+{{field-2}}) \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=${{field-4}} is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNotice that all terms in the expression $8{m}^{3}+12{m}^{2}+10m+2$ have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{even}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8{m}^{3}+12{m}^{2}+10m+2 \\\\  & =2({{4{m}^{3}+6{m}^{2}+5m+1}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=4{m}^{3}+6{m}^{2}+5m+1$ is an integer.",
